# Lecture 07 — Sep 3, 2026

## Reflection

### Displaying Search Results Clearly

- Understand how **UX design** becomes important when displaying only a small number of matches from a very large dataset.
- Avoid unnecessary scrolling when only a few results need to be shown.
- Use **indices and ellipsis/break notation** to communicate that there are additional results between displayed items.
- This showed me that presenting information clearly can be just as important as retrieving the information itself.

---

### JavaFX and FXML

- Understand the purpose of a **splash screen** in a graphical application.
- A splash screen can display information while an application is starting.
- Learn how **FXML** can be used to define the interface of a JavaFX application.
- Understand that separating the interface definition from the application logic can make a project easier to organise.

---

### File Systems as Objects

- Understand how files and folders can be represented using a **tree structure**.
- Each file or folder can be treated as an object with its own properties.
- A tree structure represents the relationship between folders and the files or folders contained within them.
- This provides a useful way to model real-world hierarchical data inside a program.

---

### Serialization and Persistence

- Understand the concept of **serialization** for saving an object's state.
- Serialization allows object data to be stored so that it can be retrieved later.
- This is useful when an application needs to preserve information between different executions.
- I understood the connection between object-oriented programming and persistent application data.

---

### Multithreading and Progress

- Understand how **multiple threads** can be used to run several jobs at the same time.
- Each job can have its own progress indicator.
- **Composite progress bars** can be used when the progress of several tasks needs to be represented together.
- Multithreading can make applications more responsive when several operations are being performed.

---

### Graceful Thread Cancellation

- Understand the idea of **graceful cancellation** when working with threads.
- Instead of abruptly stopping a thread, the application can send a signal asking it to stop safely.
- The thread can then finish its required cleanup before terminating.
- This provides a safer and more controlled way of managing background tasks.

---

### Reflection

This lecture made me think more about how applications handle information, structure, and background operations. I found the tree representation of files and folders useful because it showed how a familiar real-world structure can be represented using objects.

The discussion of serialization and multithreading also helped me understand how an application can preserve information and perform multiple tasks without relying on a single process. The UX discussion reminded me that technical functionality should still be presented in a way that is simple and understandable for the user.
